    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d75">

                <pb facs="0192" n="90" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg90"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/5757: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-01-16T16:00:00+08:00">January 16, 1933—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 16—6:44 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>54. Liu Chung-chieh, Vice Minister of Foreign Affairs, called. He stated he was
                    in Peiping to maintain liaison between Nanking and Chang Hsueh-liang and to
                    maintain contact with the Legations as Nanking realized Ministers could not go
                    to Nanking at this time. He stated that Suma of the Japanese Legation at
                    Shanghai had called on Wu Tieh-cheng<anchor n="3" xml:id="d75-f1"/> to say that
                    Japanese Foreign Office and military <pb facs="0193" n="91" xml:id="pg91"/> were
                    agreed that nothing should be done to aggravate situation and to express the
                    hope that Chinese would refrain from moving forces in the direction of
                    Shanhaikwan. Liu stated Nanking’s reply was that Japanese had taken initiative
                    at Shanhaikwan thereby aggravating situation. Nanking expressed the hope that
                    Japanese would withdraw from Shanhaikwan voluntarily.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d75-f1" n="3" xml:id="d75-f1-content">
                <p>Known also as Gen. Wu Te-chen; mayor of Greater Shanghai.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
